  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Sunny & mild conditions continue through the end\nof the week. \n\nCurrent surface analysis depicts high pressure building toward\nthe Mid-Atlantic from the Ohio Valley. Temperatures remain in \nthe mid- to- upper 70s for highs tomorrow as upper-level \nridging briefly builds over the Appalachians, with dewpoints \nranging generally in the 30s across most areas. As most of CONUS\nremains quiet off to our west, clear skies and light winds \nshould continue to dominate over the next 36-48 hours ahead of \nany potential remnants of Hurricane Isaias.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to arrive Saturday\nnight.\n\nLatest guidance show rain associated with remnants of Isaias \narriving in central VA Saturday evening, a little quicker than what \nit was shown yesterday, and spreading northeast across the rest of \nthe fcst area late Saturday night, reaching the Susquehanna River by \n12Z Sunday. The rain will be moderately intense during the overnight \nhours Sat night through Sunday morning, and begin to diminish in \nintensity mid afternoon Sunday as mid-level dry slot begins to move \nin from the southwest. Ensemble guidance shows generally one to two \ninches of rain falling, with the heaviest/most intense rainfall \nfalling in a roughly 12-hr period from 06Z-18Z Sun. Given the long \nduration and stratiform type, no flooding is expected. As for the \nrisk of severe wx, if it were to occur, it would be Sunday night, \nwith the greatest risk south of Point Lookout into the VA Tidewater \nregion. Some showers could linger into Monday morning as the mid-\nlevel circulation crosses the area. \n\nA strong cold front associated with a deepening northern stream \ntrough is then fcst to cross the area Tue night bringing autumn \nchill for the middle and second half of next week.",
